puller, maxwell_3d: met semaphores and macro DMA reads without a full drain (off by default)

Core patch. Paths are the submodule's. Needs 0210 and 0214 before it.

Source: zortos293/Lichii PR 179 (tibzejoker), head 3ce0ab7106, GPL-3.0-or-later; the wait for queued
writers comes from PR 161 (999sian), 64bb6fe0d7. Frysix traced the stale words behind 9d17457f6c.

EDEN_NX2_SEM_FAST: every acquire drains the back today, and a suspended channel sends a ring entry
per poll.
  =1 a met acquire waits only for queued engines and writes to that word; polls skip the flush on
     pages the GPU never wrote; SEM_EXECUTE no longer drains (only a warning read the result).
  =2 also reads through queued releases the front knows.
  =3 check only: works out the fast answer, logs a fast "met" the drained read denies. It does not
     try the SEM_EXECUTE change: that first runs under =1 or =2.
EDEN_NX2_DMA_WAIT_WRITERS: the same for macro DMA reads. =3 waits, =5 overlays, =4 and =6 compare
with the drained read, log, and use the drained read.

Changed from the PR, stricter:
- Queued compute, copy, inline-to-memory and 2D work runs first, so the page bits are current.
- Counter report pages go in a list of their own, not the written-page bits: here those bits send
  the guest's reads through the GPU thread (memory.cpp), and it reads report pages.
- Reads across a mapping break drain. Block-linear uploads count as writes to anywhere.
- No fast reads with EDEN_GPU_FENCE set or with macros on the back (EDEN_NX2_SPLIT=1): the front
  sees no 3D writes then. The flush skip and SEM_EXECUTE change still follow EDEN_NX2_SEM_FAST.
Changed from the PR, looser, on purpose:
- 9d17457f6c sends timed releases to the drained path too. Here they wait by ring position, because
  below High accuracy CounterReport writes a payload at once (query_cache.h). From reading only;
  =3 and =6 test it.
Still unseen: a draw's first shader store to a page.

Warning from the console: narrower waits gave no frames on Mario Kart's title and one crashed it
(docs/MKW-TITLE-FPS.md). On a Ryzen 5700G the waits moved rather than vanished, and =6 found 6
differences a run before 9d17457f6c.

Runs on the PS5: yes. Pool memory: 512 KiB with a switch on, plus a small page list.
Hang: every new wait is shorter than the drain it replaces, so none adds a block. A stale read can:
wrong draw arguments can lose the device and freeze the console. Run =3 and =6 first.
Checked: the wait and overlay code against an in-order model, front and back threads, 2 x 6 million
writes, and under ThreadSanitizer; breaking the wait makes that test fail (the overlay has no such
control). PS5-target syntax compile before the review changes, Mac-target after them. Not run.

